[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-34", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 34 — the full SF1 served resident: one-value counterpart sets cost one object; first sf1-full row 1216 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-34.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 34 - the full SF1 served resident: a one-value counterpart set costs one object\n\n> Owner, 2026-09-11: 首位を目標に co-scientist のアプローチで進めて. The\n> largest wall between here and the audited table was the footprint: the\n> full SF1 (20.6M quads) needed 10.9 GB used heap and did not serve\n> resident on a 16 GiB host (iterations 26-27, ADR-2609121100 proposed).\n> Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; SUT levi -Xmx10g, driver judah, LAN.\n\n## Generate\n\n  Iteration 27 measured that 99.5% of the counterpart sets in :eavt and\n  :aevt hold exactly one value, and that each `#{v}` is a\n  PersistentHashSet over a PersistentHashMap over a BitmapIndexedNode --\n  about 160 bytes to say `v`, most of the ~530 bytes a quad cost.\n\n  Hypotheses ranked by blast radius:\n    H1 store the value itself in place of `#{v}` -- every reader of the\n       index (engine, planner cache, entity API, the bench's attr1)\n       learns to normalise. Largest change surface.\n    H2 a set type that costs one object -- `datalog.singleton/of` is a\n       deftype that IS a set to every reader: set?, contains?, count,\n       seq, get, conj, disj, = in both directions against a real set,\n       equal hash, clojure.set, java.util.Set. conj of a second value\n       returns an ordinary set; disj of the value returns #{}. Zero\n       readers change. ClojureScript keeps #{v} (the footprint problem\n       is the JVM SUT's; a SCI deftype over the core set protocols is a\n       second thing to be wrong about in the runtime without the\n       problem).\n  H2 chosen: same saving, no reader touched.\n\n  Prediction, written first: the subset footprint roughly halves with no\n  latency change; the full SF1 fits a 10 GB heap; on the full data the\n  date-ordered reads (IC02/IC09/IS2/IC07) get slower because a person's\n  messages go from ~9 to ~300 and the ordered drive's 512-value budget\n  falls back to the plain join over 3M distinct dates.\n\n## Rank\n\n  Tests: datalog JVM 131/341/0 (levi, .cljk mirror), cljs 135/352/0 --\n  the cljs runner had been red since iteration 31 on `format` (not in\n  ClojureScript), fixed in the same commit. kotobase-peer on the new pin\n  262/804/0. New tests: the singleton against #{v} in both directions,\n  and the index storing a singleton for one value with the control that\n  two values are an ordinary set.\n\n  Footprint, same 2,004,832-quad subset, same 1,082-parameter gate (0\n  disagreements both), same served latencies (ic02 3.8/3.8, ic09 7.6/7.2,\n  is3 7.6/6.8 ms p50 before/after):\n\n    used heap   726 MB  ->  386 MB      362 -> 192 bytes/quad\n\n  Full SF1 (20,569,957 quads; 3.06M messages, 2.19M likes): loads in 14 s,\n  builds in 31 s, gate 1,082/0, READY in 3 minutes; used heap after gc\n  4,357 MB in -Xmx10g, rss 10.7 GB, swap 23% -- resident.\n\n    rung      slots  reads/s  tcr      upd/s  on-time%  thru/s   verdict\n    sf1-full    8      128    0.0002   1092    100.00   1215.8   pass (first row)\n    sf1-full    8      192    0.0002   1092     91.73   -        FAILS (ceiling)\n    sf1-full   32      256    0.00015  1475     89.44   -        FAILS (sweep began failing; no 32-slot row)\n\n  Server p50 on the full data: ic02 141 ms, ic09 113, ic07 31, is2 28,\n  is3 8, ic08/is1/is4-7 0.1-0.5, inserts 0.0-0.1. Read-after-write 234\n  checked / 0 not visible / 0 unverifiable -- the unverifiable class was\n  the subset, as the receipts said; on the full data it is 0.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Prediction held on all three parts. The wall moved: it is no longer\n    memory, it is the date-ordered reads at full scale. IC02 at 141 ms\n    means 8 threads serve ~60 IC02/s; the stream's 1,092 upd/s is what\n    makes the row read 1,216.\n  - This is M3's footprint half (SF1 whole, resident, gated). Not M3's\n    throughput half: the subset column's 4,546 ops/s becomes 1,216 on the\n    full data at 8 slots, and there is no passing 32-slot row.\n  - SF3 by the same bytes/quad is ~13 GB used heap: the next rung needs\n    a persisted plane or a larger host, not another representation\n    trick. SF30 (the first audited rung) is ~130 GB.\n  - ADR-2609121100 was 'proposed, owner decision'. The owner's\n    instruction to go for the top with this approach was taken as the\n    authorisation to measure it landed; the change is one library commit\n    with zero readers touched and is one revert away. The ADR is marked\n    accepted with the measurement; the owner can reverse it.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH by rung (SF1 whole served\nfor the first time, with both controls failing above the row). The\nsubset column is unchanged. Ratio to the audited No.1 on the new column:\n115 -- on a dataset 300x smaller than the No.1's.\n\nSeeds for iteration 35, in order:\n\n  - S25: the date-ordered reads at full scale (IC02 141 / IC09 113 /\n    IS2 28 / IC07 31 ms) -- the ordered drive's value budget against\n    3M distinct dates; per-creator date order is the likely shape.\n  - S26: a full-data parameter set (the current one is subset-derived).\n  - S23b: IC05 so INS5 has a covering read.\n  - M3 continued: SF3 needs ~13 GB -- persisted plane or host.\n"}]
